   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">i) On 30 June 2023, in follow up of the adoption of OSH Conventions into FPRW by the 110th International Labour Conference, all national trade union centres of India passed a joint resolution exhorting the Government of India for concerted national actions towards universalization of an effective OSH framework. 
The resolution called upon the Government to ratify and implement the provisions of C155 and C187. Additionally, the trade union partners emphasised the need to replace the tripartite advisory system with a statutory tripartite mechanism for national social dialogue in line with the already ratified C144.

ii) The trade union partners have implemented an innovative strategy, especially in the new context brought about by the crisis to attract women and youth to develop capacity on OSH and have a dedicated institutional structure to participate in social dialogue on OSH. Each trade union centre has formed a 5-member national Trade Union OSH Committee with one senior leader as the focal point, two sectoral leaders from priority sectors,1 woman and one youth leader.   The youth leaders in these committees have been drawn from the larger South Asia Youth OSH Volunteers network constituted in 2022-23. 
The National Committees held their first joint meeting in New Delhi on 21-22 September 2023 to develop an action plan for implementation at the local levels.

iii) Through the Workers&#x2019; Information and Support Centres (WISCs), trade Unions are able to reach workers in remote areas and identify the challenges workers face access to the Social Protection Scheme, enabling TUs to raise the issues with the state-level government. In Tamil Nadu, trade unions succeeded to enrol a group of plantation workers and women auto drivers in the schemes. In other states, the implementing TUs have similar experiences for domestic workers, sanitation workers and other vulnerable groups. i) ILO supported the joint national conference of the trade unions to develop, adopt, and disseminate the joint trade union resolution on OSH at a national workshop held in New Delhi on 30 June 2023. ILO further supported the convening of the first meeting of the national OSH committees of the trade unions. ii) A major driver of youth and women participation in the present momentum on OSH has been the ILO&#x2019;s initiative to form a South Asia network of youth OSH volunteers. ILO organized the first capacity-building workshop of this network in Kathmandu, Nepal on 27 February &#x2013; 3 March 2023 as the South Asia Trade Union Youth OSH Volunteers Camp 2023. iii) The WISC model was conceived by the ILO in response to the trade union requests for support during the COVID-19 pandemic. Since 2021, ILO has been providing active support for the establishment of WISCs at the state level with chapters operating at the district level in many states of India. Such support is further bolstered by the active engagement of the ILO with the WISCs for grassroots implementation of ILO&#x2019;s Decent Work Country Programme.</narrative>
   </description>
